WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.060
ThunderCats ranks among the best cartoons
of the '80s, alongside the likes of Masters

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.340
of the Universe and Transformers.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.480
You may not have noticed at the time, but
ThunderCats had some truly unusual touches

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.000
that read very differently to adult eyes.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.320
"Thunder, thunder, thundercats!

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.400
HO!"

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.360
ThunderCats is a bit of a sweeping epic, and
it all begins with an oft-forgotten origin

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:28.380
story.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.470
The Thundercats have to leave their doomed
home planet of Thundera, and at the time,

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:33.929
Lion-O is just a young boy.

00:00:33.929 --> 00:00:37.760
They're also mostly undressed, by the way,
but that changes quickly.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.039
Because of a long journey ahead of them, the
Cats have to spend time in suspended animation

00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:43.550
on the way to their new home.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.570
When they arrive on Third Earth, young Lion-O
finds that his body has grown into that of

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.879
a muscular adult man, but his mind is the
same.

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:53.359
"Why, I'm grown!"

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:58.270
Even though the original team includes three
actual adults, Lion-O is still the team leader

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:01.879
because he's the hereditary "Lord of the Thundercats."

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:06.590
Early on, Lion-O's immaturity and naivete
is a story point, but before long that aspect

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.010
mostly falls by the wayside.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:12.310
There are plenty of moments when Lion-O's
behavior makes more sense when you remember

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:17.280
that he's just an enormous kid, but it's almost
never made explicit after that first episode.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.369
"A fine figure of a Thundercat you’ve grown
up to be, Lion-O.”

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:22.800
“So handsome, too!"

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.359
As Thundera falls apart during the show's
premiere, the central Thundercats escape the

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:31.090
destruction under the leadership of their
wise old leader, Jaga.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.929
Jaga doesn't live to see their new home on
Third Earth, but he frequently returns as

00:01:34.929 --> 00:01:36.429
a shimmering blue ghost.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:41.540
No specific explanation for the destruction
of Thundera is given in that first episode;

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.640
it's only much later, in the third season
episode "Thunder Cubs," that we learn the

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.520
reason for the entire planet falling apart,
and it's all Jaga's fault.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.800
"Come join me for a history lesson."

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.399
Years earlier, when Jaga was a much younger
man-cat, he'd defeated a mutant leader named

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:04.929
Ratilla, who had carried a double-bladed magical
weapon, the Sword of Plun-Darr, which is basically

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:08.280
the evil equivalent to the Thundercats' own
Sword of Omens.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.250
Upon defeating Ratilla, Jaga threw the Sword
of Plun-Darr into a volcanic canyon in order

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.500
to destroy it.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:19.070
However, he underestimated the sword's evil
magic, and the Sword of Plun-Darr works its

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:22.500
way to the core of Thundera, splitting the
planet apart.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.700
Surprisingly little is made of the fact that
the wise adviser who has guided the Thundercats

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.520
all this time actually made the mistake that
cost them everything.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.120
ThunderCats largely takes place on a planet
called Third Earth, but that can't possibly

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.740
be our Earth, right?

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.280
It just looks too weird.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.470
We live on First Earth, or as we call it,
Earth.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.720
The third one must be out in space somewhere.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.750
However, if you pay attention, the show makes
clear that "Third" is a matter of time, not

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:49.750
space.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.490
This is Earth in its Third Age, when humans
are no longer the dominant species, and many

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:59.371
of the planet's inhabitants are alien colonists
and refugees from across the cosmos.

00:02:59.371 --> 00:03:03.660
This fact is made especially clear in the
episode "Excalibur," when Merlin makes a guest

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.360
appearance and reveals that the King Arthur
stories took place on the same planet where

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.480
the Thundercats now live.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.360
Merlin isn't the only ancient immortal who's
survived from First Earth to Third.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.880
The show's main villain, Mumm-Ra, falls into
the same category.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:23.150
With his glowing red eyes, deathly gray pallor,
and twisted face, Mumm-Ra is terrifying as

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:27.830
'80s cartoon villains go, which is all the
more reason that it's not entirely obvious

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:31.080
that he's the only human being in the main
cast of ThunderCats.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.430
At least, Mumm-Ra used to be a human being.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:38.150
He's an ancient Egyptian sorcerer who was
granted immortality by his gods, who he calls

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:39.870
the Ancient Spirits of Evil.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.430
Egyptian history is avoided on ThunderCats,
but it's hard to miss that Mumm-Ra is a mummy

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:49.220
who lives in a pyramid, and the Ancient Spirits
of Evil have human bodies and animal heads,

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.370
just like many gods of Egyptian mythology.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:55.790
Mumm-Ra's name is a portmanteau of "mummy,"
and the Egyptian sun god Ra.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:01.120
So, while the ThunderCats arrived from Thundera,
and the mutants come from the planet Plun-Darr,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.251
and even the Berbils arrived on Third Earth
in a spaceship, Mumm-Ra has been here all

00:04:05.251 --> 00:04:06.251
along.

00:04:06.251 --> 00:04:11.060
Of all the ThunderCats characters, he's the
one with whom we share DNA, and he's not completely

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.240
wrong about being upset at the invasion of
his planet.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.120
Early on, the powers of the Thundercats' mystical
Sword of Omens seem pretty clear.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.019
It magically extends from dagger-length into
a full sword.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:26.370
It casts a big red cat signal into the sky
to summon the other Thundercats.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:31.080
It shoots energy beams, and it allows Lion-O
to see across great distances.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.560
“Sight beyond sight!"

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.840
As the series goes on, however, the Sword
of Omens has whatever powers it needs to have

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.540
to resolve a particular plot.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.690
It can project both fire and ice at various
moments, can project solid objects like walls

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:48.690
and bridges.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:52.760
It flies, and even allows its bearer to fly,
whenever that's convenient.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.710
It even prevents the Thundercats' home planet
from exploding for a second time.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:01.380
A magical sword with many uses is fine, but
as an adult viewer, it's pretty clear that

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:05.470
the writers would just give the Sword of Omens
a new power anytime they wrote themselves

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:06.480
into a corner.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.880
"Think I'm funny do ya?

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:09.640
Snarf snarf."

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:14.070
For most of the series, Snarf is just another
in a long line of annoying little squeaky-voiced

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.740
characters who don't fit in their shows, a
lineage stretching from Scrappy Doo to Orko

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:19.800
to Screech.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.000
But Snarf once had a very specific job, acting
as Lion-O's nanny.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.320
"Just let the boy come down with a fever,
or the pip, or something, and who gets the

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.160
blame?

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.160
Poor old Snarf, that’s who.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:32.720
Snarf snarf."

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.080
When Lion-O was a kid, Snarf was employed
to watch over him and keep him safe.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:41.180
Once he has an adult body, Lion-O tells Snarf
he doesn't need that anymore, even though

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.590
he's become even more prone to getting into
trouble than ever before, and Snarf's entire

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.840
existence is pretty much rendered pointless.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.389
Snarf is a chronic worrier, which makes him
suited to babysitting, but when he doesn't

00:05:52.389 --> 00:05:57.140
have that job anymore, the same quality is
what makes him incredibly annoying.

